        <p class="profile-detail-region">Battle · Europe</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Battle of Sedan (1870)</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Date</span><span class="info-stat-val">1 to 2 September 1870; the fighting was on 1 September and the surrender was signed at 11:30 on 2 September</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Place</span><span class="info-stat-val">Sedan and the villages and woods around it (Bazeilles, La Moncelle, Floing, the Calvaire d'Illy and the Bois de la Garenne) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">The two sides</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>: the Army of Châlons under Marshal MacMahon, then Ducrot, then Wimpffen, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> present, 130,000 strong, in 202 infantry battalions, 80 cavalry squadrons and 564 guns. The Prussian Third and Fourth Armies under Moltke, with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> and Bismarck present: 222 infantry battalions, 186 cavalry squadrons and 774 guns.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The French army was surrounded and capitulated, with 104,000 men taken prisoner and 558 guns lost, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> was taken prisoner. The war went on under a new French government.</span></div>
        </div>
