    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">August 1870</span><span class="rf-text">After a hard-fought French defeat at Gravelotte on 18 August, Bazaine's Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> retreats to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and is besieged there from 19 August</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 23 August the new Army of Châlons begins a left-flanking march north-east towards the Belgian border to relieve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 30 August the Prussian Fourth Army defeats MacMahon at Beaumont, and he withdraws towards Sedan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">August 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 31 August the French deploy around Sedan while Moltke arranges his armies to encircle them</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 1 September the Germans attack from several sides, take Bazeilles, drive the French into the Bois de la Garenne and defeat the breakout attempts</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 2 September Wimpffen signs the surrender of the Army of Châlons and the fortress of Sedan</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 4 September the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> collapses in a bloodless revolution and a Government of National Defence is proclaimed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">September 1870</span><span class="rf-text">On 19 September the Prussian Third and Fourth Armies begin the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-sedan-1870" data-v="6c210b2d04" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The French held a ring round Sedan</b> MacMahon placed the 7th Corps on the north-west crest, the 1st Corps facing east and the 12th Corps in Bazeilles, with the 5th Corps in reserve in the centre. The Germans closed a ring round the north-west, west and east.</li><li><b>The Bavarians storm Bazeilles</b> A Bavarian brigade crossed the Meuse on pontoon bridges in the morning mist and rushed Bazeilles. The French fought from stone houses while Bavarian guns shelled the buildings.</li><li><b>The 8th Division arrives and Bazeilles is stormed again</b> The Prussian 8th Infantry Division arrived at 08:00. Von der Tann had not been able to bring artillery to bear from long range, so he committed his last brigade to storm the town, supported by guns across the Meuse.</li><li><b>Wimpffen attacks the Saxons at La Moncelle</b> Ducrot ordered a retreat but Wimpffen overruled him and threw his forces against the Saxons at La Moncelle. The French rallied briefly, drove back the artillery around the village and pressed the Saxons.</li><li><b>The French counter-attack collapses</b> With Bazeilles taken and fresh waves of Prussian troops arriving, the French counter-attack began to collapse. The 8th Infantry Division was sent to reinforce the fighting at La Moncelle.</li><li><b>The 22nd Division turns Douay's left flank</b> Douay's left flank was dug in above Floing and its fire kept the Germans out of the village. By 13:00 the French artillery there had been defeated and the German 22nd Division turned the flank, defeating French counterattacks.</li><li><b>French cavalry charge at Floing</b> Ducrot sent Margueritte's cavalry to open an escape route with three attacks on Floing, where the XI Corps stood. Margueritte was mortally wounded in the first charge and German infantry fire defeated the other two.</li><li><b>German infantry reach the Calvaire and the French break</b> By 14:00 German infantry stood on the Calvaire and attacked from all sides. The 7th Corps broke, seeking refuge in the Bois de la Garenne or Sedan, and the 1st Corps was routed by Saxon and Guards artillery.</li><li><b>The Guards take the Bois de la Garenne</b> The Bois de la Garenne was under German artillery fire from several sides. When the Prussian Guards infantry captured the wood at 14:30, the French troops inside it surrendered.</li><li><b>A last attack after the white flag</b> Napoleon III hoisted a white flag on the walls of Sedan. Wimpffen refused to surrender and launched one more attack, which the Germans defeated.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii.jpg" alt="Napoleon III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandre_Cabanel_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexandre Cabanel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After a pyrrhic victory at Gravelotte on 18 August 1870, Marshal Bazaine's Army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, 150,000 strong, retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, where 168,000 Prussian troops of the First and Second Armies besieged it from 19 August. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and Marshal MacMahon formed the new Army of Châlons on 17 August to march to its rescue. From 23 August, with the emperor personally leading the army and MacMahon in attendance, it marched in a left-flanking movement north-east towards the Belgian border, meaning to avoid the Prussians before striking south to link up with Bazaine.</p><p>The Prussians had repeatedly outmanoeuvred the French through August, and the march depleted the French forces and left both flanks exposed. Leaving the First and Second Armies at Metz, Moltke took the Prussian Third and Fourth Armies northward and caught the French at the Battle of Beaumont on 30 August. MacMahon lost 7,500 men and 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a> there. He abandoned the planned link-up and ordered the army to withdraw north-west towards Sedan, an obsolete 17th-century fortress.</p><p>His intention was to rest an army worn by a long series of marches, resupply it with ammunition and, in his words, manoeuvre in front of the enemy. He underestimated the German strength and believed the hills around Sedan would give a major defensive advantage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i.jpg" alt="Wilhelm I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kaiser_Wilhelm_I_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wilhelm Kuntzemüller, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Army of Châlons was 130,000 strong. At the opening of the battle it had 202 infantry battalions, 80 cavalry squadrons and 564 guns. It faced the surrounding Prussian Third and Fourth Armies, which together had 222 infantry battalions, 186 cavalry squadrons and 774 guns. The Fourth Army, under Generalfeldmarschall Helmuth von Moltke, was accompanied by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-i">Wilhelm I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>.</p><p>On arriving near Sedan on 31 August MacMahon posted Douay's 7th Corps to the north-west on the crest between the Calvaire and Floing. Ducrot's 1st Corps faced east, and Lebrun's 12th Corps garrisoned Bazeilles. General Wimpffen, newly arrived, took over 5th Corps from Failly, whose unit had been routed at Beaumont, and it was placed in reserve in the centre. The fortress covered the French rear, and the Calvaire d'Illy offered hills and woods for defence. MacMahon refused Douay's request to dig trenches, saying the army would not stay long at Sedan.</p><p>Moltke divided his forces into three groups: one to hold the French where they were, another to race forward and catch them if they retreated, and a smaller one to hold the river bank. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> XII Corps crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>, with the Prussian Guards on its right. The I Royal Bavarian Corps under von der Tann moved on Bazeilles, with two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridges</a> laid across the Meuse by Bavarian engineers. The Prussian V and XI Corps completed the encirclement to the north-west by 09:00 on 1 September.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The fighting opened at Bazeilles. The French 1st Corps had barricaded the streets and enlisted the help of the townspeople. Von der Tann sent a brigade over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontoon-bridge">pontoon bridges</a> at 04:00 in the early mist, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> took the village by surprise. French Marines fought back from stone houses until Bavarian artillery shelled the buildings into rubble, and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">brigades</a> of the 1st, 5th and 12th Corps arrived. Bazeilles was taken at 08:00, and the artillery reached it at 09:00.</p><p>Fighting spread to La Moncelle, the one point where a breakout seemed possible. MacMahon was wounded and named Ducrot to command, who learned of it at 07:00 and ordered a retreat. Wimpffen, who held a government commission to succeed MacMahon if he were disabled, overruled him almost at once and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> at La Moncelle. This brought a brief French rally, which collapsed as fresh Prussian troops arrived. By 11:00 Prussian artillery had taken a toll, and a French cavalry charge was defeated by infantry and artillery fire.</p><p>German fire drove the Army of Châlons north into the Bois de la Garenne, where it was surrounded. Wimpffen ordered a breakout to the south at 13:00, but it failed. Margueritte's cavalry made three charges at Floing; he was mortally wounded in the first, and the French lost 791 killed and wounded by 15:00. The 7th Corps broke and Ducrot's 1st Corps was routed. The Prussian Guards took the Bois de la Garenne at 14:30, and the French inside surrendered. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> then hoisted a white flag on the walls of Sedan, which Wimpffen first refused to accept, launching one more attack that the Germans defeated.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Moltke saw the white flag and sent Bronsart von Schellendorf to learn what it meant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> sent General Reille back with a letter of surrender for the Prussian headquarters on the hillside above Frénois, and Wilhelm and Bismarck read it and Bismarck accepted it. Wimpffen asked for an honourable capitulation and for prisoner-of-war status for the army. Moltke refused, pointing to the French lack of ammunition and food, to 250,000 German troops against the 80,000 fighting for the French, and to the German position all round. The truce was extended until 09:00 on 2 September, and at 11:30 that day Wimpffen signed the surrender of the army and the fortress.</p><p>The Germans lost 9,860 men: 1,310 killed, 6,443 wounded and 2,107 missing. The Army of Châlons lost 3,220 killed and 14,811 wounded, with 104,000 taken prisoner and 558 guns, and the Germans also took 1,000 wagons and 6,000 horses. The French soldiers marched in heavy rain to an improvised prisoner-of-war camp, where they starved for the next week. On 3 September Napoleon III left for a comfortable captivity at Schloss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergpark-wilhelmshohe">Wilhelmshöhe</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>.</p><p>Two days after the news reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, a group of protesters stormed the imperial palace and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> collapsed in a bloodless revolution. The Government of National Defence that replaced it carried on the war for five more months before becoming the Third Republic. The Prussian Third and Fourth Armies besieged Paris from 19 September.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>MacMahon's stated intention at Sedan was to rest and resupply the army and, in his words, manoeuvre in front of the enemy. When Wimpffen sought an honourable capitulation, Moltke answered with the French lack of ammunition and food and with a count of 250,000 German troops against 80,000 fighting for the French. That count stands beside the 130,000 given for the Army of Châlons as a whole.</p><p>The French command passed from MacMahon to Ducrot to Wimpffen in the course of the morning.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The defeat at Sedan, the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s second army, with the first French army shut up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, is held to have sealed the outcome of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s favour. The new French government fought on, so the surrender ended the Empire and not the war. The Government of National Defence proclaimed on 4 September carried the war on for five more months before becoming the Third Republic.</p><p>In recognition of the part the battle played in German unification, many Germans celebrated Sedantag, the Day of Sedan, on each 2 September until 1919. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaiser">Kaiser</a> himself refused to declare it an official holiday, and it remained an unofficial day of celebration. Napoleon III spent his captivity at Schloss <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergpark-wilhelmshohe">Wilhelmshöhe</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, and the French prisoners watched him leave on 3 September with disinterest.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>On 1 and 2 September 1870 the Prussian Third and Fourth Armies surrounded the French Army of Châlons at Sedan, and 104,000 French soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> were taken. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">Second Empire</a> fell on 4 September, but the war went on under a new government.</em></p>

